THE ALGORITHM (ORIGINAL mpd implementation; the SUL-1.0-derived old core was never opened)

  Two repairs, both proved from the file's own bytes, neither ever guessed:

  wrapped-line  A pair (i, i+1) of the replacement block is an eligible site IFF both entries are
                non-empty, entry i+1 carries NO indentation of its own, entry i OPENS a bracket or a
                quote, and entry i+1 is a NET CLOSER returning the delimiter state to exactly what
                entry i started from. Rejoin with one space after a token needing a right operand
                ([,+-*/%=<>&|?!:]$), otherwise with nothing — so `call(one,` + `two)` -> `call(one, two)`,
                `foo(` + `bar)` -> `foo(bar)`, `"hello ` + `world"` -> `"hello world"`.

  paired-indent EVERY non-empty entry flush left while the replaced range carries >= 1 indent, block
                spanning >= 2 entries. Each entry takes the indent of the replaced line it stands in
                for (k-th <- k-th); a block that grew keeps the range's last indent.

  THREE REFUSALS — the whole point of the word "conservative":
    (1) block EQUALS the range it replaces  -> return untouched (the exact-match clause)
    (2) MORE THAN ONE eligible wrap site    -> repair neither, splice the block exactly as written
    (3) append / prepend                    -> never reach the pass (no range to prove against)

  Scanner: ()[]{} depth + double-quote and backtick quotes with backslash escapes, line-agnostic.
  A SINGLE QUOTE is deliberately NOT tracked (string delimiter in some languages, apostrophe in prose);
  leaving it out can only make a line look LESS open, and an open line is only ever a CANDIDATE.

